insert into public.tips (id, unit_id, title_en, body_md, status) values
  ('2ee7b356-9d31-5af9-a191-7b3049f6870d', '8ee9449f-672c-5e88-91cb-6db07525f370', 'Disculpá, permiso', '**Disculpá** gets a stranger''s attention: *Disculpá, ¿sos Martín?* **Permiso** is what you say to get past someone, on a full bus or in a doorway.', 'draft'),
  ('23fd59cf-939b-51f2-9dd8-5626bcb20afa', 'eba40ebe-93cf-52fb-a853-9a2836300617', 'When you get lost', 'Stuck on a word: **¿cómo se dice…?** — *how do you say…?* — and **¿qué significa?** — *what does it mean?* Too fast: **¿me repetís?** or **más despacio, por favor**. **No entendí** is *I didn''t get that*. And the line that buys you patience: **hablo un poco de castellano** — Argentines call the language *castellano*.', 'draft'),
  ('52586356-7f20-5175-b293-6a0dd0d21654', 'bf7b8307-3d3a-57b2-9e3b-06bca38b4ef1', 'Tengo una reserva', 'At the front desk one line does it: **tengo una reserva a nombre de** … and your surname. They will ask for your **pasaporte**. Staying two nights? *Dos noches.*', 'draft'),
  ('a9ac5ecc-ccab-51b8-a6d1-c8e2e314d67b', 'bf7b8307-3d3a-57b2-9e3b-06bca38b4ef1', 'Check-in, check-out', 'Hotels and hostels use the English words: **el check-in** and **el check-out**. To ask the time: *¿A qué hora es el check-out?*', 'draft'),
  ('c584c6c4-0148-5cf4-9a92-48b82350a4e0', 'bf7b8307-3d3a-57b2-9e3b-06bca38b4ef1', '¿Está incluido?', 'Ask before you pay: *¿El desayuno está incluido?* *¿Hay wifi?* *¿Hay toallas?* An **alquiler temporario** is a flat you rent for a few days or weeks — what the booking apps offer all over Buenos Aires.', 'draft'),
  ('f8d95867-18ac-54cd-a860-30ed3a2df254', '4db2a92e-ad85-5b0f-88e2-71629dbf00d2', 'No cash on the bondi', 'You can''t pay the driver in cash. You tap a **SUBE** card on the reader — on buses, the subte and trains. You load it at a kiosco or a subte station: *¿Me cargás la SUBE?* When the **saldo** runs out, load it again.', 'draft'),
  ('e03a678a-dcdb-5f01-a285-b2b87d91bb92', '4db2a92e-ad85-5b0f-88e2-71629dbf00d2', 'Tell the driver where you''re going', 'On a colectivo the **boleto** — the fare — depends on how far you go, so you tell the driver first (*hasta Palermo*) and then tap. Not sure it''s the right bus? *¿Va a Palermo?*', 'draft'),
  ('621d77e5-9002-54ab-a910-a40fe5970eb3', '4db2a92e-ad85-5b0f-88e2-71629dbf00d2', 'Me bajo en la próxima', '**Me bajo en la próxima** — *I''m getting off at the next stop*. Don''t know the stop? *¿Cuántas paradas faltan?* or simply *¿Me avisás?* In the subte, changing lines is a **combinación**, and you wait on the **andén**.', 'draft'),
  ('591fcf6e-f9e9-552f-aefe-b86b51a8b85e', 'd9917aa3-1a7b-5287-9437-9c652db27797', 'Two streets, not a number', 'Porteños give the driver a corner, not a house number: *Voy a Thames y Gorriti.* Near the end you guide: *acá a la derecha*, **dejame acá**, *en la esquina está bien*.', 'draft'),
  ('7e126434-68df-561d-b3c2-e370396414d6', 'd9917aa3-1a7b-5287-9437-9c652db27797', 'Taxi, remis, app', 'A taxi is black and yellow; you stop it in the street when the red *libre* sign is lit: *¿Está libre?* A **remis** is a car you book by phone at a fixed price. Most people now use an app: *pido un auto por la app*. A taxi driver is a **tachero**.', 'draft'),
  ('a5722513-f14d-54a4-ad42-967c6daabe18', 'd9917aa3-1a7b-5287-9437-9c652db27797', '¿Cuánto sale hasta…?', 'With a remis, ask the price before you get in: *¿Cuánto sale hasta el aeropuerto?* In a taxi the meter decides. Paying cash, people round up: **quedate con el vuelto** — *keep the change*.', 'draft'),
  ('36daec09-756b-57d1-a508-f8ba61fd0b2c', '72bb9993-19e7-5dad-bdc7-78b4161e7045', 'The numbers to call', 'In Buenos Aires **911** is the police and **107** is the ambulance. In the street, shout **¡ayuda!** and tell someone what to do: **llamá a la policía**, *llamá a una ambulancia*. *Es urgente.*', 'draft'),
  ('00f64f36-241e-5c70-ad23-4d93e605066f', '72bb9993-19e7-5dad-bdc7-78b4161e7045', 'Me perdí, me robaron', 'Learn these two whole — they are in the past, which the course teaches later. **Me perdí** — *I got lost*; right now you are **perdido** or **perdida**. **Me robaron** — *I got robbed*: *me robaron el celu*.', 'draft'),
  ('c61da1fd-3bea-5819-848d-a327403ee480', '72bb9993-19e7-5dad-bdc7-78b4161e7045', '¡Cuidado!', '**¡Cuidado!** is *watch out!* — a car, a step, a wet floor. To get help fast, say what you need: *necesito un médico*, *¿hay un hospital cerca?*', 'draft'),
  ('5670c80f-9ebc-5f2c-aebb-37ff6ce7e2e2', 'c15b7bfe-bc72-5bf8-a990-5ba1799a2d79', '¿Débito o crédito?', 'The question at every till. With **crédito** comes a second one: *¿en cuántas cuotas?* — how many monthly payments. *En una cuota* is all at once; **sin interés** means no extra charge. Not sure they take cards? *¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?*', 'draft'),
  ('bea55b21-7ab2-5676-ac8b-6831fc7ad8ee', 'c15b7bfe-bc72-5bf8-a990-5ba1799a2d79', 'Te transfiero', 'Paying by bank transfer is normal, in shops and between friends: **te transfiero**. You ask for the **alias**, the short name of the other person''s account. Or you scan a **QR** with your phone — most people use the Mercado Pago app for it.', 'draft'),
  ('b197e033-1ea3-59a5-be8d-e704941efbf5', 'c15b7bfe-bc72-5bf8-a990-5ba1799a2d79', 'Lucas and dólares', 'Prices run to thousands, so a thousand pesos has a short name: **una luca**, *diez lucas*. To **cambiar dólares**, first ask the rate: *¿A cuánto está el dólar?* And cash often gets a better price: *¿Hay descuento en efectivo?*', 'draft'),
  ('440071f8-57d1-514e-b230-35edae4c7045', 'fa7ef0ed-4de1-5b37-9c23-1d5747ae531d', '¿Me pasás tu número?', 'This is the normal way to ask for someone''s number — friendly, not pushy. They may say **agendame** — *save my number* — and read it out. You answer: *te agendo*.', 'draft'),
  ('57dbf66a-6d96-5de2-a203-beadc2a925ee', 'fa7ef0ed-4de1-5b37-9c23-1d5747ae531d', 'Te escribo', 'Everything runs on WhatsApp: plans, shops, even the doctor. **Te escribo** — *I''ll text you*; **mandame un mensaje**; **llamame**. On Instagram: **seguime** — *follow me*.', 'draft'),
  ('a807dff0-189b-5406-b3c0-73d7d4b65c1f', 'fa7ef0ed-4de1-5b37-9c23-1d5747ae531d', 'Chip y datos', 'For a local number you buy a **chip** — a SIM card — at a phone shop or a kiosco, and then you load **datos**: *cargar datos*, the same verb as loading the SUBE. *No tengo datos* means no internet until the next wifi.', 'draft'),
  ('884f42b5-2708-5009-ac9c-58b4265070d5', '4aaf79fa-f71f-5712-8a98-5facae7b4221', 'Understand first', 'You will hear these words every day — in traffic, at the football, between friends. Learn to understand them first. Say them only with close friends who already swear with you. Never to a stranger, at work, to someone older or to the police.', 'draft'),
  ('312fb178-1035-5102-b74c-28c2d9e736b2', '4aaf79fa-f71f-5712-8a98-5facae7b4221', 'Swearing at things', 'Most swearing is aimed at the situation, not at a person: **¡la puta madre!**, **¡mierda!**, **¡carajo!**, **¡qué cagada!** A bad day is *un día de mierda*. Two are harmless: **una boludez** is something silly or small, and **me cagué de risa** means *I laughed a lot*.', 'draft'),
  ('da8de45b-718a-598c-b598-e48991bb7640', '4aaf79fa-f71f-5712-8a98-5facae7b4221', 'Swearing at people', 'These hurt. **Pelotudo** is stronger than *boludo* and rarely friendly. A **forro** is a nasty person. An **hinchapelotas** won''t stop bothering you: *¡no rompas las bolas!* — often just *¡no rompas!* **Andá a cagar** is *get lost*: a joke between friends, a fight with a stranger.', 'draft')
